11. Test data: UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot, FlyBase, SGD, TIGRFAMs and SMART
===============================================================================

What: the tests of LOSAT (the directory LOSAT/tests/ of the repository) hold sequences, annotation
texts and database rows of other projects. They are in the repository and in the source
archives that GitHub makes from it. They are not in the crate, the pylosat wheel or the release
archives of the binaries. The file LOSAT/tests/THIRD_PARTY_DATA.md of the repository lists them with
the files that hold them and the changes made, and says which data need no attribution (Pfam,
NCBI's own data, INSDC, Ensembl, the Protein Data Bank). The data that do:

- UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot (UniProt Consortium, https://www.uniprot.org/): sequences and
  definition lines of Swiss-Prot records. Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International
  (CC BY 4.0), https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ ; terms:
  https://www.uniprot.org/help/license . UniProt's statement: "We have chosen to apply the
  Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0) License
  (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/) to all copyrightable parts of our
  databases. All databases and documents in the UniProt FTP directory may be copied and
  redistributed freely, without advance permission, provided that this copyright statement
  is reproduced with each copy." The records are written into test inputs and databases, some
  with an added second definition line or a shortened title.
- FlyBase (https://flybase.org/), through Ensembl BDGP6.54: five Drosophila melanogaster
  cDNA records (FBtr0078009, FBtr0100310, FBtr0083245, FBtr0301286, FBtr0078640) and the
  definition line of a sixth (FBtr0337025), added to a zebrafish record. FlyBase
  distributes the annotations and data it produces under CC BY 4.0
  (https://wiki.flybase.org/wiki/FlyBase:About).
- SGD, the Saccharomyces Genome Database (https://www.yeastgenome.org/), through Ensembl
  R64-1-1: three protein records (YLR106C, YBR142W, YOL075C). CC BY 4.0, as SGD's help pages
  state it; SGD's own terms page could not be read directly when this was written
  (2026-11-21).
- TIGRFAMs, through NCBI's Conserved Domain Database (CDD) v3.22: the description text of 29
  TIGRFAMs families in 10 excerpts of CDD's cddid.tbl (tests/fixtures/rpsbproc/), taken from
  NCBI's FTP site on 2026-11-11. The rows are excerpts and otherwise unchanged, except that
  four of the files cut the description to 24 characters. License: Creative Commons
  Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-SA 4.0),
  https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/ (full text:
  https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode). NCBI holds the rights since
  April 2018 and publishes the data under it
  (https://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/hmm/TIGRFAMs/license_and_availability.txt). The TIGRFAMs rows
  stay under CC BY-SA 4.0. NCBI asks that use of TIGRFAMs cite these articles:
    Haft DH, Loftus BJ, Richardson DL, Yang F, Eisen JA, Paulsen IT, White O. TIGRFAMs: a
    protein family resource for the functional identification of proteins. Nucleic Acids Res.
    2001 Jan 1;29(1):41-3. PMID: 11125044
    Haft DH, Selengut JD, White O. The TIGRFAMs database of protein families. Nucleic Acids
    Res. 2003 Jan 1;31(1):371-3. PMID: 12520025
    Selengut JD, Haft DH, Davidsen T, Ganapathy A, Gwinn-Giglio M, Nelson WC, Richter AR,
    White O. TIGRFAMs and Genome Properties: tools for the assignment of molecular function
    and biological process in prokaryotic genomes. Nucleic Acids Res. 2007 Jan;35(Database
    issue):D260-4. Epub 2006 Dec 6. PMID: 17151080
    Haft DH, Selengut JD, Richter RA, Harkins D, Basu MK, Beck E. TIGRFAMs and Genome
    Properties in 2013. Nucleic Acids Res. 2013 Jan;41(Database issue):D387-95. doi:
    10.1093/nar/gks1234. Epub 2012 Nov 28. PMID: 23197656
- SMART (https://smart.embl.de/), through CDD v3.22: the description text of 12 SMART
  families in 8 excerpts of cddid.tbl (same rule as above), and the smart rows of CDD's
  bitscore_specific.txt. No SMART hidden Markov models or alignments are in the tests. SMART
  states that its data (hidden Markov models, alignments and thresholds) are available via a
  license that is free to academics and not to commercial users
  (https://smart.embl.de/about.cgi); it states no terms for the description text of a family.
Files: LOSAT/tests/THIRD_PARTY_DATA.md lists them.
License: the licenses named above; LOSAT's own tests are under the MIT License.

